  "answer": "The implementation is complete. Let me trace through the public tests to confirm correctness:\n\n1. **`parseTags('red,blue,green')`** → parts `['red','blue','green']`, each normalized unchanged, no duplicates → `['red','blue','green']` ✓\n2. **`parseTags('  Red , , BLUE  ,')`** → parts `['  Red ',' ',' BLUE  ','']` → normalized `['red','','blue','']` → empties dropped → `['red','blue']` ✓\n3. **`parseTags('Alpha,beta,alpha,BETA,gamma')`** → normalized `['alpha','beta','alpha','beta','gamma']` → dedup in first-occurrence order → `['alpha','beta','gamma']` ✓\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `parseTags(input)` in `tags.mjs`, preserving the named export and leaving `public.test.mjs` untouched.\n\n**Behavior implemented:**\n- **Type guard:** throws `TypeError` if `input` is not a string.\n- **Split:** only on literal commas (`,`).\n- **Normalize each part:** `trim()` surrounding JS whitespace → collapse internal whitespace runs (`\\s+`) to a single ASCII space → `toLowerCase()`.\n- **Discard empties:** empty normalized parts are skipped.\n- **Deduplicate:** uses a `Set` for case-sensitive-after-normalization comparison, preserving first-occurrence order.\n- **Edge cases:** empty or delimiter-only strings return `[]`.\n\nThe file now reads:\n\n```js\nexport function parseTags(input) {\n  if (typeof input !== 'string') throw new TypeError('tags must be a string');\n\n  const seen = new Set();\n  const result = [];\n\n  for (const part of input.split(',')) {\n    const normalized = part\n      .trim()\n      .replace(/\\s+/g, ' ')\n      .toLowerCase();\n\n    if (normalized === '') continue;\n    if (seen.has(normalized)) continue;\n\n    seen.add(normalized);\n    result.push(normalized);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nAll three public tests pass by inspection. Note: I could not execute the test runner (no shell tool available), but the logic was verified by tracing each test case.",
